# 106 · A rig on Apple silicon that uses its GPU

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.3.0](../roadmap.md#v030) · **Tracked in:** [GitHub issue 87](https://github.com/iammrduncan/thermite/issues/87), the source of truth for this work. This file keeps its context.

Built on 2026-11-13 and proven on one Mac. It waits for the owner's hands-on look. **"The handoff" is this goal's [Handoff](#handoff) section:** to pick the work up, on this machine or another, start there.

## What

A rig on a Mac with Apple silicon uses the Mac's GPU. The rig stays what a rig is, a harness container and its supervisor with the same wall, and the GPU reaches the harness as a Vulkan device.

- **How the GPU gets there.** Containers on macOS run in a Linux virtual machine. One kind gives its containers the Mac's GPU: a Podman machine made with the libkrun provider. The container sees `/dev/dri`; Mesa's Venus driver in the container carries Vulkan to the Mac, where it becomes Metal.
- **What it serves.** Engines with a Vulkan back end. It does not serve MLX, which needs Metal: that is a runner on macOS itself, goal [279](279-runner-on-macos-for-mlx.goal).
- **What was built,** each part in Progress:
  1. A target, `vulkan-gpu`, beside `cpu` and `cuda-gpu`.
  2. The harness on a Vulkan base, and the rig's device setup, fence and isolation check for a GPU that is `/dev/dri`.
  3. The one-machine install on a Mac: `thermite doctor` and `thermite local init` say whether the Mac's machine gives its GPU, and `local init` writes the compose file that gives it to the rig.
  4. A smoke kit for the target, which the judge's smoke check runs.

The goal as first written (2026-10-23) planned a rig on macOS itself, without containers, for MLX. The study of 2026-11-13 found the way through Vulkan, which keeps the rig's wall as it is, and the owner chose it. The rig on macOS itself is goal 279 now.

## Why

The owner, 2026-10-23, on the one-machine install: a machine that is a Mac should give its rig the Mac's unified memory and its Apple silicon GPU, for MLX and work like it. For v0.1.0 the install said plainly that a Mac's rig gets the virtual machine's CPUs and memory and no Apple GPU ([037](_completed/037-gpu-compose.goal)).

On 2026-11-13 the owner wanted a second, local-only Thermite on a Mac for trying v0.3.0, with the Mac's own GPU for small models.

## Done when

Written by the builder on 2026-11-13, when the build began.

- [x] **The target.** A rig with `THERMITE_TARGET=vulkan-gpu` names its device (a name, a driver, a UUID, an id that stays the same), and is ready for timing only when Vulkan lists a real GPU. A rig that sees only the software device, or none, says so in words and measures nothing.
- [x] **The wall holds.** On a rig with a Vulkan GPU, the roles that must not use the GPU can't open its device nodes, and the isolation check proves it for each role and each node, on the real machine.
- [x] **The install.** On a Mac, `thermite doctor` and `thermite local init` say whether Podman's machine gives the GPU, and what to do when it doesn't. When it does, `local init` writes `compose.gpu.yaml`, and the stack started with it has a rig that reports the GPU to control.
- [x] **The judge measures on it.** The smoke check of that rig, asked through control, passes with the Vulkan smoke kit: each candidate gets its expected verdict from the real judge, on the Mac's GPU.
- [x] **The pages and the credits** say what exists.
- [ ] **The owner has tried it,** hands-on.

Not in this goal:

- MLX and Metal work, which need a runner on macOS itself: goal [279](279-runner-on-macos-for-mlx.goal).
- A released Vulkan harness image. The image builds from the source: goal [280](280-released-vulkan-harness-image.goal).
- A limit on the GPU's memory. The owner, 2026-11-13: none is needed (Decisions).
- A rig with a Vulkan GPU set up by control on a machine or a cluster, more than one Vulkan GPU in a rig, and a development GPU of the agents' own. A Mac has one GPU and runs the one-machine install.
- Other GPUs through Vulkan on Linux (AMD's, Intel's). The target's code does not name Apple, but nothing else is tested. AMD GPUs are goal [105](105-amd-gpus.goal).

## Decisions

- 2026-11-13, the owner, asked what GPU the local-only Thermite on a Mac should use for trying v0.3.0: the Mac's own, with very small models for tests, and a rig image that can do MLX and Metal work. So this goal was wanted, for small models first. No step of [v0.3.0, step by step](../v030_release_plan.md) holds it: it was built beside the steps, at the owner's command of the same day.
- 2026-11-13, the owner, later the same day: find how the Mac can give about 10 GB of its GPU to a runner. Others have surely solved it; Apple's container tool was named as a place to look. The study of that day is in Progress.
- The builder's note on a rig image, of that day, before the study: an image alone can't give a rig the Mac's GPU, since a container on macOS runs in a Linux virtual machine that can't reach Metal. The study then found the one kind of machine that carries Vulkan to it.
- 2026-11-13, the owner, asked whether to run the proof (krunkit and its parts from Homebrew, a second Podman machine, one small model): yes, at once.
- 2026-11-13, the owner, when the build began: one Podman machine on libkrun holds both control's containers and the runner's; the small Qwen model of the proof is the test model, and it never fills the Mac's memory. So:
  - **One machine.** Control, the bus and the rig run in the one libkrun machine. Thermite makes no machine of its own: the machine is the Mac owner's, and `local init` and `thermite doctor` read it.
  - **No limit on the GPU's memory.** The limit of about 10 GB, asked for earlier, is not built. The Vulkan device gives no figure to hold a limit to, and the owner's test models are small.
- The builder's choices, 2026-11-13. Each can be changed:
  - **The way through Vulkan,** and MLX apart (goal 279), as the study's "What fits Thermite as it is" says.
  - **The target is named for what it reaches through, `vulkan-gpu`,** not for Apple: its driver reads any Vulkan device and refuses the software one. Only a Mac's GPU is tested.
  - **The image is built on a distribution's own Mesa,** Ubuntu 26.10's 26.2, pinned by digest. The proof used the machine's makers' patched Mesa; the build then found that Mesa 26.2 and newer open the GPU as they come, and older ones do not (Progress). No third party's package source is in the image.
  - **The fence alone keeps roles off the GPU.** In a rootless container on the machine the broker can't give the device nodes a group, so the layout is the shared one with the Landlock fence, which a rig with NVIDIA GPUs already has for that case. The fence got one new rule, for `/dev/dri`.
  - **The judge can't ask whether the GPU is quiet.** The device reports no load, memory in use or temperature. The target is ready when the GPU is listed; a disturbed measurement shows in the run's own repeats and its noise figure.
  - **On a Mac where Docker and Podman both answer, Podman is the rig's engine** when its machine gives the GPU, unless `--engine` names another.

## Open questions

- Whether a run on this GPU needs a memory budget of its own later, for larger models: the device reports the Mac's whole memory as its own, and the Mac's other work shares it.
- Whether the same target serves AMD and Intel GPUs on Linux, whose Mesa drivers the image also carries. Not tested; goal [105](105-amd-gpus.goal).
- Whether the checks can cover this path without a Mac. Today they hold the parts (the driver on real `vulkaninfo` text, the device nodes and the fence's rule, the compose file, the kit's shape); the GPU itself needs a libkrun machine, which no CI runner has.

Settled on 2026-11-13: a container with Vulkan through the virtual machine was worth the look, and it is what was built; the wall on macOS itself, and whether that is one piece of work with goal [277](277-runner-without-a-container-engine.goal), moved to goal 279.

## Handoff

Written on 2026-11-13, at the owner's word, so that the work can go on from another machine. Everything needed is on the branch `v0.3.0`; this section says where the work stands and how to get a rig on a Mac's GPU running again.

### If you were asked to pick up the handoff

"The handoff" is this section. Whoever is asked to pick it up, a person or an agent in a new session, on any machine, does this:

1. **Be on the right code.** Check out the branch `v0.3.0` and pull. This file on that branch is the newest state: read its Done when, Decisions and the last entries of Progress.
2. **Say where things stand,** in a few lines, from "Where it stands" below, and ask the owner which machine this is: a Mac with Apple silicon, where the rig can run on the GPU, or another machine, where only the code and its tests can be worked on.
3. **On such a Mac, get the test Thermite running** by "Get it running on a Mac with Apple silicon" below, and tell the owner its address and where its token is (never print the token).
4. **Go on with "What is left, in order".** The first two items are the owner's own look and a first campaign with agents, so they are done with the owner, not alone. What the owner wants changed is changed in this goal, with its tests and its pages, and recorded in Progress.
5. **Close the goal only at the owner's word.**

What a new session may not know, and must keep to:

- **v0.3.0 runs on a local-only test Thermite,** on the owner's own machine. It is not put on a live control until the owner says so ([v0.3.0, step by step](../v030_release_plan.md)).
- **Nothing here starts a campaign, installs a program or changes a machine without the owner's yes.** The steps below install krunkit and make a Podman machine: ask first, and say that the Podman machine that runs must stop.
- **The record says what the owner decided in the writer's own words,** with the date, in goal files, issues, pull requests and commit messages. It does not quote the owner.
- **One goal at a time.** This goal is finished, with the owner, before another is started.

